---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const stats = [
  ["1882", "born at Rosario de Santa Fe on 22 February — from his own passport", "/passport/"],
  ["1911", "married at St Dominic's, Wynberg, six years after their first son was born", "/taylor/"],
  ["1931", "plans lodged for stables at Koeberg Road, Rugby", "/naairs/"],
  ["£15,527", "the gross value of his estate in 1950 — roughly half a million pounds today — including £26 owed for carrots", "/estate/"],
  ["2,124", "people bear this surname in the whole world", "/name/"],
  ["1879–1901", "every year of the Rosario baptism index, L and Ll read end to end — twenty-two years, forty-one frames, and not one Lerena in any of them", "/searched/"],
  ["Juan Carlos", "the name of his father — from an army card filed at the Cape, found 10 September 2026", "/enrolment-card/"],
  ["125", "people of this surname met along the way, recorded and searchable — fourteen connected, seven excluded, the rest honestly unplaced", "/register/"],
];
---
<Base title="The Lerena Archive"
      eyebrow="Rosario · the Cape · the Rand · Brisbane"
      dek="A horse-dealer out of Argentina, a stable under Table Mountain, and four family traditions tested against the record rather than repeated."
      bare={true}>

<section class="hero">
  <img src={u("/art/banner.webp")}
       alt="A painted impression of a stable yard on the sand flats north of Cape Town, Table Mountain in the distance, two horses standing in the open."
       loading="eager" decoding="async" />
  <div class="cap">
    <div class="kick">A family history, kept honestly</div>
    <h1>Three generations back, a horseman out of Rosario.</h1>
    <p class="sub">Rosario · the Cape · the Rand · Brisbane — a family that kept Spanish names for eighty years in a country that had no use for them.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">A modern painted impression, not a photograph, and not a picture of any building this family is known to have owned — it stands for the yard at Rugby recorded in the 1931 building file. The signature in its corner is the image generator's invention.</p>

<section class="opener">
  <p class="lede">
    This archive was built for <b>David Defranceski</b>, born in Johannesburg, living in Brisbane — and it
    runs backwards from his mother: to <b>Germiston</b>, where her father died; to <b>Wynberg</b>, where her
    grandparents married in 1911 and their sons were baptised; to <b>Newlands</b>, where the horseman died
    in 1950; and to <b>Rosario de Santa Fe</b>, where his own passport says he was born and where the
    registers do not yet agree.
  </p>
  <p class="lede" style="margin-top:14px">
    It is a public archive with one rule: <b>the living are left out</b> — omitted, not hidden. And a
    second rule, which is harder: every claim says how well it is known.
    <span class="chip doc" style="padding:2px 8px;font-size:10px">Documented</span>
    <span class="chip inf" style="padding:2px 8px;font-size:10px">Inferred</span>
    <span class="chip lore" style="padding:2px 8px;font-size:10px">Family lore</span>
  </p>

  <div class="doors">
    <a class="door" href={u("/direct-line/")}>
      <div class="dn">Follow the line</div>
      <div class="dd">Three generations from <b>Pablo Armando Lerena</b> to David's mother, with a record
        behind every step and no inferred link left in the descent.</div>
      <div class="go">Start at Rosario →</div>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href={u("/passport/")}>
      <div class="dn">Read the passport</div>
      <div class="dd">Série A No. 07962, issued at Cape Town on Christmas Eve 1940. A birthplace, a
        birthday, and a profession in one word: <b>Trainer</b>.</div>
      <div class="go">See what it settles →</div>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href={u("/hypotheses/")}>
      <div class="dn">See what is unproved</div>
      <div class="dd">Every proposition the archive is entertaining but has not proved — what suggests it,
        what would settle it, and the two already retired as wrong.</div>
      <div class="go">Open questions →</div>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
    <div class="kick" style="margin-bottom:8px">10 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">His father has a name.</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:10px">An Argentine army enrolment card — <em>clase de 1882</em>,
      born at <b>Rosario de Santa Fé</b>, enrolling office <b>Capetown</b> — names
      <b>Juan Carlos Lerena</b> and <b>María Lerena</b> as the parents of
      <b>Pablo Armando Lerena</b>. It had been sitting behind a machine-made index that spelled him
      <em>Armand Lorena</em> and placed him 1,400 km away in Salta.</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Corroborated from the other side of the ocean: his eldest son,
      named in the 1950 estate, was <b>Ricardo <em>Juan Carlos</em> Lerena</b>.
      <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>Read the card →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the family remembered, and how it has held up</h2>
  <p class="dek">Four traditions were handed down. Three now have documents; one has evidence against it.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:15rem">The claim</th><th>Where it stands</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>An Argentine great-grandfather, from Rosario</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Confirmed</span> His own passport gives <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe,
            22 February 1882</strong>, and records him as Argentine <em>by birth</em>. The tradition was
            exactly right. <a href={u("/passport/")}>The passport →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He brought horses</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Confirmed</span> <strong>Trainer</strong> on his passport;
            <strong>horse-dealer</strong> at his marriage; the Argentine <strong>6th Cavalry</strong> in
            1915; <strong>Race Horse Owner</strong> at his death; a 1931 file for <strong>stables</strong>
            at Rugby; a yard that took apprentices; and, among his debts, a claim for
            <strong>£26 of carrots</strong>. <a href={u("/horses/")}>The horses →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He was Consul General</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip inf">Disputed</span> Nothing supports it. On the 1940 passport the consular
            signature belongs to A. B. Bayne; Pablo Armando is the bearer. The family <em>did</em> hold an
            Argentine consular passport, which may be how the story began — and the family's own note says
            the post may have been <strong>Uruguayan</strong>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He had an ostrich farm</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip lore">Doubtful</span> The deeds registers show him holding no land at all,
            and every document places him in Cape Town racing rather than the Karoo. Not disproved —
            Oudtshoorn is unsearched, and a farm could have been leased.
            <a href={u("/south-africa/")}>South Africa →</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Eight numbers</h2>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:24px">
    {stats.map(([n, l, h]) => (
      <a class="cardlink" href={u(h)}>
        <div class="nm" style="font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums">{n}</div>
        <div class="txt">{l}</div>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Where it stands, and where it stops</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The line is closed and documented from <b>Pablo Armando Lerena</b> — born 1882, died at Newlands in
      1950 — through <b>Nuno Fernando Lerena</b> to David's mother. His wife <b>Mary Septima Taylor</b> can
      be traced four generations further, to a <b>James Taylor born about 1796</b> in Suffolk, and a family
      settled at Bideford and Northam in north Devon.</p>
    <p>Above him the line now runs one generation further, to <b>Juan Carlos Lerena</b> and
      <b>María Lerena</b>, on his own army enrolment card. That card also does what no other document had
      managed: it <b>corroborates the passport from outside the family</b>, in Argentine hands, giving the
      same year and the same birthplace. <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>The card →</a></p>
    <p>What still stops is the baptism. The Rosario index has been read <b>letter by letter, under any
      surname, for 1881, 1882 and 1883</b>; and on 10 September 2026 the <b>L and Ll sections were read
      end to end, every year from 1879 to 1901</b> — forty-one frames, verified folio by folio. There is
      <b>no Lerena, no Llerena, no Larena and no Lorena in any of them</b>. Until 1888 the cathedral was
      the city&rsquo;s only parish. A boy born there in February 1882 to a father named Lerena ought to be
      in that book, and is not. That is now a sharp question rather than a blank wall: he was baptised
      somewhere the index does not reach, or not as an infant — or the index, a later typescript compiled
      from the registers, is itself incomplete. <a href={u("/searched/")}>The sweep →</a></p>
    <p>And one thing the card does <em>not</em> settle, said plainly: <b>María is written with her
      husband&rsquo;s surname</b>. Whether she was a Lerena by birth — which would make the marriage one
      between kin — or was entered under her married name, the card cannot tell. Her maiden name is the
      next thing to find.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">Corrections are the most valuable thing anyone can send. A
    family member with a date, a photograph of a document, or a flat contradiction of something written
    here will change this archive, and the change will be recorded rather than quietly made.</div>
</section>
</Base>
